s the f50 a bow so powerful and  fast it can reach speeds of up to 60  miles per hour  hannah mills is the most successful  female sailor in olympic history and now  she races for great britain in the sale  gp championship  what type of skills does it take to make  it go so fast to go fast i guess  everyone in the boat has their  individual role that's very different i  guess from each other so obviously the  driver is the driver you know i'm  calling the wind and the race course  which way we want to go then there's the  guy that's doing the the flight controls  yep you heard right these boats really  do fly  and it's all down to these blades called  hydrofoils  so these are the hydrofoils yes so these  are the things under the boat that make  the boat fly out the water and get to  the speeds that we can get to but when  we go sailing they come up and down and  they go down sort of two two and a half  meters so that's how high the boat is  flying out the water and you can kind of  see the shape you know they're kind of  shaped like a wing obviously they're  pointing inwards rather than outwards  that you'd imagine an airplane but it  does the same thing i then got to see  hannah and the team in action on their  first practice day in plymouth  they're going so fast and flying through  the water because of the hydrofoils  underneath the boat  that was so cool i mean i felt like we  were flying through the water i mean i  looked over and they were like running  from one side it was so exciting they  were like skimming across the top  and it's great to see the teams are a  mix of men and women  cell gp hope to inspire the next  generation of young female sailors like  catty rogers who came up through their  youth program just last year and has  already got to sail with team gb  the cell gp program has developed the  women's pathway program which is trying  to close the gap between male and female  athletes  so it's basically trying to give female  athletes more time on the water and just  equal opportunities basically it's  amazing now you're here to inspire other  young people like yourself well that's  the aim for sure sadly it wasn't a win  for team gb this time but it was great  to see them in action  [Music]  here at fyi we discuss the big issues  with the kids who are affected by them  we call it conversation  when i say refugees what do you think of  ukrainians fleeing the war right  but charities are appealing to us not to  forget the millions of other refugees  who have fled from other wars and are in  terrible conditions  so we're gonna head to one of the  world's largest refugee camps for a  special report  [Music]  my name is bana i'm 13 years old and i  live in turkey but this isn't where i'm  from  my home city is aleppo in syria  sadly my happy childhood was torn apart  by wall  as the bombs fell around my home  [Music]  my mom and i reached out to the world  using social media to let people know  what was happening  so many people saw my messages  it changed my life forever  [Music]  the first time i got to know what was  going on in syria was when i saw  banner's videos posted online i remember  being really worried about what would  happen to her  especially after she told the world that  her best friend yasmin had been killed  in a bomb attack  i am sad  so bad  it was way too dangerous for banner and  her family to stay in syria  so they fled to turkey where they became  refugees  but bannon wasn't like any other refugee  her social media posts had caught the  attention of millions of people  including the turkish president richard  type erdogan  wanting to show the world his kindness  he arranged a safe home for her and her  family thank you  sadly not all refugees have been made to  feel as welcome as banner was  many syrian families ended up living in  terrible conditions in refugee camps  so banner decided to use her global fame  to demand better treatment of refugees  i'm about to have a conversation with a  girl determined to change the world a  girl who is campaigning for the one  thing that would end the suffering of  millions of kids  peace  [Music]  the first time i met banner was back in  2019  my job was to introduce her at a  conference in dubai as she gave a speech  to celebrities and world leaders  your excellencies  ladies and gentlemen please welcome to  the stage banner  alibaba  her message was that refugees should be  able to live with dignity and children  who've had to flee their countries  should still be able to go to school  they want to be teachers they want to be  doctors but without education they want  to be anything  even though we live in different  countries fanner and i have become  friends and today we're meeting up in  jordan brayden hi hello how are you i'm  very well thank you bana how are you i'm  good  but we haven't come to jordan just for a  catch-up banner has asked me here  because she wants to show me why her  campaign to help refugees is still so  desperately needed  we're on our way to a place called  zachary the largest syrian refugee camp  in the world  it's just six miles from the border with  syria  so britain have you ever been to a  refugee camp before no i haven't and to  be honest with you i'm not really sure  what to expect okay  this place opened ten years ago and it  was just a hundred of tents in the  desert and it was just meant to be  temporary but now it's home to over 80  000 people and more than half of them  are children  the saturday refugee camp is huge it's  like a city in the middle of the desert  and this is the main street  it's not what i was expecting at all  so brandon what are your first  impressions then well for me it just has  to be the size of this place i mean it's  massive and i think it is a little bit  overwhelming and to think none of this  was here 10 years ago i feel like a  temporary solution has almost become an  obvious permanent exactly i mean just  look at this street this has become a  proper shopping street and i've heard  people have been calling it the  champs-elysees which is one of the [ __ ]  shopping streets in paris it's a joke  look around you can pretty much buy  anything here bicycles food  even phones wow i know and wedding  dresses  isn't that crazy people getting married  here i mean i want one i mean even  though we are in a refugee camp it's  almost like everybody's just getting on  with their everyday lives and trying to  make the best out of a bad situation  to find out what life is like for the  refugees here we're meeting a family  with six children  they came here in 2012 to escape the war  and have been here ever since  so why was it so important for you and  your family to leave syria and come here  surya can't be haram in syria there was  a war and no safety  if we'd have stayed in syria there would  have been no safety at all  we came to jordan because the jordanians  are kind people  so what are your hopes for the future  it's been there no i hope to continue my  studies outside the camp and outside of  jordan and succeed and learn english  hello what are your hopes for the future  and where do you want to go  [Music]  i want to leave and not stay here  i want to leave to a european country  there is no future here  and can you please tell me what is the  best thing about living in a camp and  what is the worst thing  the best thing  there is nothing  and the worst thing  is that we are living here  that's what she is the worst thing is  the dust  [Music]  most of the people live like this  basically  metal containers  i mean even though the families are  trying to make it like a proper home and  have that warmth to it i just can't get  my head around how difficult it must be  to come to a completely different place  and have none of your stuff yeah when  you become a refugee you can only take  what you can carry i still remember when  i had to leave syria i couldn't take my  dolls i was really upset  this looks quite basic but you know it  can be a lot worse  not far from the family's home there's a  community centre where children can come  and meet their friends log onto laptops  and get involved in all sorts of  activities i'm meeting two girls who  come here every day so can you remember  how old you were when you first came to  the camp  i was three years old and i came  and there were tents but things have  changed a lot since then  life has changed  and instead of tents  they're in our caravans  you can do whatever you want now  it is much better than it used to be  with possibilities to study  are you allowed to you know leave the  camp sometimes and then come back what  is the freedom like i started  if you have permission then you can come  and go as you like  but there are police and security on  if we go and get permission for a  holiday  for say a week  and stay out of the camp for longer than  that  then we won't be allowed back in  many of the refugees dream of a day when  they can return to their homeland and  live in peace  but syria's civil war has been going on  for more than 11 years now with  different groups fighting over who  should have control of the country  more than half a million syrians have  been killed and more than 13 million  people have fled their homes to seek  safety  most of them come to neighboring  countries like turkey where banner lives  or jordan  for its part the uk is taking in around  20 000 syrian refugees  [Music]  some syrian people are so desperate that  they try to get into the uk on small  boats crossing the english channel from  france  but some people think they shouldn't be  allowed to stay here  back in jordan about 80 percent of  syrian refugees live outside the  official camps but with very little  support many of them struggle with  extreme poverty and it can be especially  dangerous for children  [Music]  you know it makes me really upset seeing  children living in tents it's just not  right it makes me feel really guilty  that i have a proper house and a great  school  but at the same time i'm trying my best  to highlight about the syrian refugees  lately the ukrainian people have been  getting a lot of help this is just a  great thing but people shouldn't also  forget about refugees around the world  it's time for us to leave  we've seen for ourselves just how  difficult life can be for syrian  refugees but at the same time we've seen  how there are also moments of happiness  for the children who live here  people aren't just around feeling sorry  for themselves they're doing the best  they can to make things better  and maybe one day with the help of  charities and campaigners like banner  they'll be able to live a normal life in  a place they're proud to call their home  [Music]  join in on the big conversation about  racism poverty conflict and war and how  we are affected for more conversations  head to sky kids on demand or our  webpage first.news fyi just hit the link  see you next time

